From: Robin Snyders <robin@snyders.xyz>
Date: Wed, 12 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] NFC: nci: report refused configuration parameters

CORE_SET_CONFIG_RSP carries a status byte followed by the identifiers of any
parameters the controller refused. nci_core_set_config_rsp_packet() reads only
the status, so a configuration can be reported as applied while individual
settings were dropped and left at their defaults. On a handset that is the
difference between an RF front end that is tuned for the board and one that is
not, and nothing in the log distinguishes the two.

Log the refused identifiers. The parameters are printed byte by byte because
proprietary identifiers are two bytes wide and the response gives no way to
tell the widths apart.

Signed-off-by: Robin Snyders <robin@snyders.xyz>
---
diff --git a/net/nfc/nci/rsp.c b/net/nfc/nci/rsp.c
index 9eeb862..f49ed4a 100644
--- a/net/nfc/nci/rsp.c
+++ b/net/nfc/nci/rsp.c
@@ -169,6 +169,23 @@ static void nci_core_set_config_rsp_packet(struct nci_dev *ndev,
 
 	pr_debug("status 0x%x\n", rsp->status);
 
+	/*
+	 * A controller may accept the command and still refuse individual
+	 * parameters, listing them here. Nothing else reports that, so a
+	 * configuration can appear to apply while leaving settings at their
+	 * defaults.
+	 */
+	if (skb->len > 1 && rsp->num_params) {
+		unsigned int i, n = skb->len - 2;
+
+		if (n > rsp->num_params)
+			n = rsp->num_params;
+
+		for (i = 0; i < n; i++)
+			pr_warn("NCI: config parameter byte 0x%02x refused\n",
+				rsp->params_id[i]);
+	}
+
 	nci_req_complete(ndev, rsp->status);
 }
 
